Kept the check as a small conditional. Equal counts pass, while unequal counts fail. This also handles the case where both counts are zero, since they are still equal.

```python
class Solution:
    def unique_binary_tree(self, a, b):
        if a <= b and b <= a:
            return True
        return False
```

Time complexity: O(1)

Space complexity: O(1)

The absolute difference gives the number of extra nodes in one traversal count. There can be no extra nodes, so the answer is true only when that value is zero.

Edge cases such as one empty traversal and one nonempty traversal return false. Two empty counts return true because their sizes match.

```cpp
class Solution{
    public:
        bool uniqueBinaryTree(int a, int b){
            int difference = a - b;
            if (difference < 0) {
                difference = -difference;
            }
            return difference == 0;
        }
};
```

Time complexity: O(1)

Space complexity: O(1)

The two traversal descriptions must contain the same number of nodes. If their sizes differ, they cannot describe the same tree. A direct comparison is enough here.

```cpp
class Solution{
    public:
        bool uniqueBinaryTree(int a, int b){
            return a == b;
        }
};
```

Time complexity: O(1)

Space complexity: O(1)

This version checks the difference between the two counts. A difference of zero means both traversals have equal length, which is required before trying to build one tree from them.

Dry run: for `a = 5` and `b = 5`, the difference is `0`, so the method returns `true`. For `a = 4` and `b = 5`, it returns `false`.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean uniqueBinaryTree(int a, int b) {
        int difference = a - b;
        return difference == 0;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: O(1)

Space complexity: O(1)

Both counts are valid together only when the smaller count is also the larger count. This avoids any loop or collection and keeps the method constant in size.

A useful detail is that the values are compared without changing either input.

Dry run: with `a = 7` and `b = 6`, the smaller value is `6` and the larger is `7`, so the result is `false`. With `7` and `7`, both values match and the result is `true`.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean uniqueBinaryTree(int a, int b) {
        int smaller = Math.min(a, b);
        int larger = Math.max(a, b);
        return smaller == larger;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: O(1)

Space complexity: O(1)
